      "narrative": "Maricopa County’s residential market is in a cooling-but-still-active phase, with prices essentially flat to slightly down and longer marketing times giving buyers more leverage than in recent years.[2][4][10] Recent July 2026 data show modest year-over-year increases in closed sales alongside declines in pending sales and new listings, while inventory is in the mid-teens of thousands of active listings and months of supply around the mid-3 range, indicating a balanced-to-tilting-toward-buyer market rather than the extreme seller’s market of the pandemic era.[6][10] Local reports describe sellers cutting list prices as homes sit on the market longer, median or average days on market in July ranging roughly from the high-50s to 70+ days, while median prices hover around the mid-$400,000s, reflecting a plateau after the prior run-up.[2][6][10] Phoenix-area dynamics continue to be shaped by large employers and in-migration to the region’s tech, manufacturing, and service sectors, but higher mortgage rates and affordability pressures are now tempering demand and contributing to the more negotiable conditions buyers are experiencing.[4][11][13]",
